{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And also the nation, etc.</b> It is difficult: how can Scripture say ״וְגַם״ (\"and also\") concerning what preceded — namely the enslavement and the affliction — when this is a benefit, namely taking vengeance upon our enemies? Second, the word <b>et</b> is superfluous. Third, His saying <b>I will judge</b> — it is well known that He Himself is the judge. Fourth, why did He interrupt between the event that HaShem decreed upon his children through the judgment of the enslavers, and it would have been fitting to complete the matter and say ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן יֵצְאוּ״ etc., which is the conclusion of the matter of Israel, and afterward say ״אֶת הַגּוֹי אֲשֶׁר יַעֲבֹדוּ״ etc.? And for this it is possible that the intention is to say by this arrangement that after HaShem judges the nation, they will go out with great substance, and He took the order as it actually happened — just as we find that so it was: first He judged them, and afterward they lent them great substance." "<b>However,</b> the meaning of the verses will be explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanḥuma, Beshalaḥ) on ״וַחֲמֻשִׁים עָלוּ״ etc. — one out of fifty, and some say one out of five hundred, and the rest died in Egypt during the days of darkness and gloom so that the Egyptians would not perceive them. They further said of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah, ch. 14) on the verse ״וּלְכָל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל הָיָה אוֹר״ etc., that Israel would search the treasure-rooms of the Egyptians and they would know [what was there], etc., and through this means they lent them and they despoiled [them], etc. — and see what I explained regarding this verse in its place. And this is that HaShem said to Abraham <b>וְגַם אֶת</b> etc. — meaning: besides the decree of enslavement and affliction, there is also an additional decree of punishments He will bring upon Israel when He judges the Egyptians — to destroy and kill a large portion of them, according to one authority fifty parts, and according to another authority five hundred parts. And through this the word <b>וְגַם</b> (\"and also\") and the word <b>et</b> are precisely accounted for. And His saying <b>I</b> — meaning: this plague the Egyptians did not know of, for He alone did, because it was during the days of darkness. And adjacent to this He said <b>וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן</b> (\"and afterward\") etc. — meaning: through the means of that judgment, when He brings that plague to judge both of them — the nation that enslaves and the nation that is enslaved — which is the plague of darkness, afterward they shall go out with substance etc., as said above, for through this means they lent them." "<b>Further,</b> the verse will be explained by what we may further examine: why did HaShem bring ten plagues upon the Egyptians, and upon the sea they were smitten, etc., after we find that the One who decreed had so decreed?" "<b>And behold,</b> the Rambam, of blessed memory, in the Book of Knowledge (Hilkhot Teshuvah, ch. 6, halakhah 5) wrote that the reason the subjugating nations are punished is because HaShem did not say [to enslave] a specific nation, etc. And the Ramban and the Ra'avad objected to him, for what of it — after all, they fulfill a commandment by executing the king's decree. And the Ramban explained that the punishment is because they added to afflict more than the measure of labor that HaShem had decreed, and according to his words the nations are punished only for the excess affliction. And according to his approach, of blessed memory, the verse is explained in this manner: <b>And also the nation I will judge, I</b> — meaning, HaShem will judge His people, and from the conclusion of the matter you know that [the Egyptians] far exceeded the measure of the decree. And He was precise in saying <b>et</b> — meaning, He will judge it together with it, it itself in its glory and its very essence. And He says <b>I</b> — for He alone knows the measure of the decree and the measure of the excess for which He will punish them. And all this is according to the approach of the Ramban." "<b>But the correct interpretation</b> in my eyes is that all the nations are obligated to be punished for all the affliction and suffering that they cause us, and the investigation [of whether they acted under divine dispatch] is fundamentally unwarranted. For the reason the nations afflict us is because of their separating us from them and our fulfilling the commandments that we observe — and go out and learn: if Israel had been idol worshippers and had been a single people with the Egyptians, the Egyptians would not have enslaved them and they would have been like their Egyptian brothers. And so in every nation and nation that enslaves Israel and does evil to them, the reason is because we are the heritage of Shaddai. From this, what room is there for the claim that they are the emissaries of the decreeing El? After all, El decreed concerning their transgressing one branch of His word, while they do evil [to us] because we do not transgress the entire Torah, [because] we are not like all the nations, the house of Israel. And this is what He said (Zechariah 1:15): ״אֲנִי קָצַפְתִּי מְעָט״ — meaning: because they transgressed a portion of the Torah I was angry a little; but the nations who discipline Israel — on the contrary, through the means of affliction they assist in causing [Israel] to transgress everything. And so it is with one who apostatizes: they exempt him from all evil and do him good." "And this is the verse's saying <b>וגם את הגוי</b> — \"I Myself will judge\" — for I know the thoughts of man and his devices, and the reason for the affliction: it is not for the sake of fulfilling the commandment of HaShem that He decreed upon them. And from the implication you learn that the enemy acted with great wickedness, since he afflicted them out of hatred — and there is no hatred like hatred of the faith — and it is obvious that he would be cruel toward them exceedingly. Accordingly, it is fitting to say <b>וגם את</b> etc., which is a declaration of the multiplication of the suffering that will pass over his children. And adjacent to this it writes <b>ואחרי כן יצאו</b> etc. — meaning, since their intention is not to fulfill the commandment of HaShem, they are included in the restitution of the theft for all that they enslaved them. Go forth and learn from the incident of Geviha (Sanhedrin 91a), who demanded of the Egyptians wages for all the years they enslaved Israel." "<b>Further</b>, the verse alludes in the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Megillah 29a): ״They were exiled to Egypt — the Shekhinah was with them״ etc., and it is written (Psalms 91:15): ״עמו אנכי בצרה״. And further they said regarding the verse (Exodus 24:10): ״ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר״ — that it alludes to the bondage with which Israel was enslaved in bricks etc. And this is the verse's saying <b>וגם את הגוי אשר יעבודו</b> — with them there, together with them, <b>דן אנכי</b> — alluding to the Shekhinah that would be with them in exile. And through this the word <b>וגם</b> is precise, for it is an addition of suffering, and the word <b>את</b> and the word <b>אנכי</b>."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:51.736018864Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}